  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe strategy employs a multi-resolution hierarchical optimization approach to tackle the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. Starting with a coarser resolution allows for an initial exploration of the solution space before refining to higher resolutions, capturing both large-scale structure and fine adjustments. This method helps escape local optima common in derivative-based methods. The optimization at each level uses convex relaxations to guide the search and employs feasibility repairs to ensure constraints are met.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h as a piecewise constant function over N intervals. Define h_i = h(i/N) for i=0..N-1. Each h_i \u2208 [0,1]. The integral constraint becomes sum(h_i) = N/2. The overlap integral at lag k is computed as the convolution (h * (1 - h))(k), scaled by N/2. The objective is min_h max_k (h * (1 - h))(k) * (2/N). \n\nFor each resolution level, define a surrogate problem where h is expressed in terms of parent solution via upscaling. This maintains continuity across scales and leverages information from coarser grids.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe procedure follows these steps:\na. Validate the initial_h_values, if provided, and use it as a starting solution at the coarsest grid.\nb. Begin with a coarse grid (N=14) and gradually refine to N=70.\nc. At each level, perform optimization using:\n   i. Convex relaxation to find a lower bound on C5.\n   ii. Gradient-guided local search to minimize overlap.\nd. Use feasibility repair to project solutions back into valid [0,1] bounds and satisfy the integral constraint.\ne. Track the best solution across all levels, ensuring it meets evaluation criteria.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nOptimize starting with N=14 (coarse), then 28, then 70 (fine). At each level, allocate 400s, 400s, and 300s respectively. Use upscaling with linear interpolation from parent solutions. At each refinement, the initial guess is the parent solution, allowing transfer of structural features to higher detail. If no parent is available, initialize with uniform h=0.5 * N/2 / (2.0/(N)).\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nAllocate 400s per refinement level, totaling ~1200s, slightly under budget.uga\nImplement safeguards:\n- If optimization fails, revert to previous best.\n- After each step, verify feasibility and objective score.\n- Store checkpoints to resume if interrupted.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEnsure:\n- h \u2208 [0,1] for all points.\n- sum(h) = N/2.\n- C5 is computed accurately as max(correlation(h, 1-h) * dx).\nReturn the best solution found, with its computed C5 and N.",
